      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "3f9f3e88-b151-5e0c-b4e9-c964484b860d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Karlheinz Pintsch, Rudolf Hess, Adolf Hitler, Fredric Warburg, Lobsang Rampa, Pierre Boulle, William Shirer\n- Products: Mein Kampf, The Third Eye, The Bridge over the River Kwai, The Rise and Fall of the Third Reich\n- Places: Scotland, United Kingdom\n- Roles: Deputy Führer\n- Events: May 1941 flight to Scotland, World War II, imprisonment, conviction, suicide\n- Topics: historical debate, publishing\n\nFacts:\n- In 1933, Rudolf Hess was appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler.\n- Rudolf Hess edited Adolf Hitler's book Mein Kampf.\n- In May 1941, Rudolf Hess flew solo to Scotland to try to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch reported Rudolf Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Adolf Hitler.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch's recollections and notes about Rudolf Hess's May 1941 flight have been debated by historians.\n- Rudolf Hess served as Deputy Führer until 1941.\n- Rudolf Hess was taken prisoner, later convicted of crimes against peace, and served a life sentence until his suicide.\n- Fredric Warburg published notable works including The Third Eye by Lobsang Rampa, The Bridge over the River Kwai by Pierre Boulle, Mein Kampf by Adolf Hitler, and The Rise and Fall of the Third Reich by William Shirer.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "cdac0be2-b62c-52fc-816c-3f584fd8ac9b",
        "text": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.\nPage: Mein Kampf. Sentence: The book was edited by Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess.\nPage: Fredric Warburg. Sentence: Other notable publications included \"The Third Eye\" by Lobsang Rampa, Pierre Boulle's \"The Bridge over the River Kwai\", Adolf Hitler's \"Mein Kampf\" and William Shirer's \"The Rise and Fall of the Third Reich\".",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
